course: adult-life
lesson: 60
title: Next time: switch off, tell a grown-up, and parental controls
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The best way to handle an accident with an adult film is to know what you will do before it ever happens. Fire drills work the same way: when you have practised, your body knows the steps even when your heart is racing.
explain: There are only two steps: switch off (or look away and walk out), and tell a grown-up you trust. You don't have to explain everything; one sentence is enough: "I saw something not for kids."
explain: Vivid example: in Auckland, Freya and her dad sit on the sofa with the TV menu open. Dad shows her how her own profile has her picture, a fern, on it; how to turn autoplay off; and that 13+ films now ask for a PIN only grown-ups know. "And if something gets through anyway?" asks Dad. "Off, then tell you," says Freya. "Sweet as," says Dad.
explain: Grown-ups help with tools: kids profiles, autoplay switched off, safe search, PINs and report buttons. No tool is perfect, so the two steps still matter, and an accident never changes who you are.
fact: The two steps are: switch off or walk away, then tell a trusted grown-up.
fact: If the remote is lost, the power button on the TV itself, or on the wall, switches it off.
fact: Autoplay can be turned off in the settings of most video and streaming apps, so the next video doesn't start by itself.
fact: A kids profile, usually marked with its own picture, shows only films for younger ages; you can ask for your own profile on a relative's TV too.
fact: Parental controls can ask for a PIN before films above a chosen age, such as 12 or 13+, will play.
fact: Safe search is a setting that filters out adult results when you search, though it can miss some.
fact: Adverts and videos can be reported with a grown-up using the app's report button.
fact: Checking the rating on a box, a poster or a streaming page before pressing play prevents many accidents.
fact: "I don't want to see that" and "That's not for me" are polite, firm sentences you can always use, with friends or older children.
fact: At a sleepover you can always ask to ring home if a film is too old for you.
fact: Slow breathing, such as breathing in while counting to four and out while counting to six, helps calm a racing heart.
fact: A bad picture fades faster if you fill your head with something else, like a favourite place, a game or a song.
fact: Telling quickly is easier than telling later; you can tell any trusted grown-up, and you can still ask questions days later.
fact: An accident does not change who you are.
exercise: Freya plans what she'll do if she lands on an 18 film again. What are the two steps? | Switch off, then tell a grown-up | Keep watching, then tell a friend | Turn it down, then keep it secret | Change channel, then forget about it
exercise: Alfie and Dad turn off autoplay. What will that change? | The next video won't start by itself | The tablet will only ever play cartoons | Videos will stop halfway through | The tablet won't need charging
exercise: Grace asks her aunt for her own kids profile on visits. Why is that smart? | It only shows films for her age | It makes her aunt's films load faster | It lets her watch her aunt's films free | It stops her aunt using the TV at all
exercise: Jake learns which profile is his by the picture. Why does that help? | He won't land in his brother's profile by mistake | Pictures make the films on the profile shorter to watch | The picture shows which films are the funniest | Only profiles with pictures can play cartoons
exercise: Ruby practises saying "I don't want to see that." Why practise it? | So the words come easily when she needs them | So she can say it to her teacher every day | So she can get out of watching any film at all | So her friends think she is very grown up
exercise: Harry wants to play a DVD from Grandma's old box. What should he check first? | The rating on the box | The colour of the disc | The length of the film | The price on the sticker
exercise: At a sleepover the older kids put on a film that's too old. What can Poppy do? | Ask to ring home or go to another room | Watch it so she doesn't seem a baby | Hide under the duvet and say nothing | Tell the older kids she's seen worse
exercise: An 18 film comes on and Max can't find the remote. What should he do? | Press the power button on the TV itself | Keep looking for the remote while watching | Throw a cushion at the screen to stop it | Wait for the film to finish by itself
exercise: Sophie's friend holds out her phone: "Watch this bit!" It's an 18 film. What can Sophie say? | "That's not for me," and look away | "Okay, but only for a second" | "Send it to me so I can watch later" | Nothing, it's rude to look away
exercise: An advert for an 18 film keeps popping up in Theo's game. What can he and a grown-up do? | Report the advert with the app's report button | Click the advert to make it go away forever and ever | Nothing, adverts are always allowed to show | Delete every game on the tablet for good
exercise: Lily learns how safe search works. What does it do? | It filters out adult results, though not perfectly | It blocks every website except for the school ones | It makes the search much faster on the tablet | It checks spelling so you type the titles of films right
exercise: Oscar's heart races after an accident. What can calm it down? | Slow breathing: in for four, out for six | Running up and down the stairs very fast | Holding his breath for as long as he can | Watching another scary film to get used to it
exercise: A bad picture keeps coming back to Evie. What helps it fade? | Thinking about something nice, like a favourite place | Trying very hard to see the picture clearly again and again | Staying awake all night so she can't dream it | Drawing the picture so that she never forgets it
exercise: Finn switched off and told Mum, but a week later he has a question. Is it too late to ask? | No, it's fine to ask questions later | Yes, questions must be asked that same day | Yes, Mum will be cross he brought it up | No, but he should ask his friends instead
exercise: Ava learns that telling quickly is easier than telling later. Why? | A secret gets heavier the longer you keep it | Grown-ups only listen on the same day it happens | Late telling always gets you punished | Things you saw disappear after a day
exercise: Leo learns one sentence to use. Which works best? | "I saw something not for kids." | "I did something very bad today." | "Nothing happened, I'm just tired." | "Can I have my own TV, please?"
exercise: Mia learns about parental controls with a PIN for 12+ films. What does the PIN do? | Only someone who knows it can play those films | It makes those films cheaper to rent or buy | It stops the TV from playing any films at all | It tells the cinema which films Mia has seen
exercise: Noah worries the accident has changed him. What's the truth? | An accident does not change who he is | He is now a different and worse person | He has to tell the whole school about it | He can never watch any film again
test: Isla's mum is at work. Something not for kids comes on at Nan's. Who can Isla tell? | Nan, or any trusted grown-up | Nobody at all until Mum gets home | Only a teacher at school | Only her best friend
test: Charlie writes the two steps on a sticky note for the fridge. Which note is right? | 1. Switch off. 2. Tell a grown-up. | 1. Watch it. 2. Tell a friend. | 1. Turn it down. 2. Keep quiet. | 1. Hide. 2. Forget it happened.
test: Ella explains to her little brother: "If something yucky comes on, you just shut your eyes and wait." Put her right. | Switch it off or walk out, then tell a grown-up | She's right, shutting your eyes and waiting is enough | He should just turn the sound right down | He should call his friends to come and see
test: Big cousin: "Parental controls are pointless, nothing gets past them anyway, so you don't need the two steps." What's wrong? | Controls help, but none is perfect, so the steps matter | He's right, controls block absolutely everything bad, every time | Controls are only for grown-ups, not for kids | The two steps only work when there are no controls
test: Dad on the phone to the TV company: "Yeah, I need to set a PIN for anything over a 12, the kids use it." What is he doing? | Setting parental controls with a PIN | Ordering a new TV for the kids' room | Cancelling the TV because it's broken | Asking for more 18 films to be added
test: Mum, cross: "Bloody autoplay, I swear I turned it off." What might have happened? | The setting was turned back on or never saved | Autoplay can never be turned off in any app | The tablet chose to disobey Mum on purpose | Mum must have pressed play by accident twice
test: Your mate: "If I tell my dad, he'll know I was on my brother's profile. I'll just keep quiet." What would a good friend say? | Tell him; it's easier now and it was an accident | Keep quiet forever and he'll never, ever find out | Tell your brother instead, he won't mind | Delete the whole profile so nobody knows it happened
test: Little brother, panicking: "The remote's gone and the scary film's still on!" What should you tell him? | Press the button on the TV, or come and get a grown-up | Keep searching the sofa while the film is playing loudly | Shout at the TV until it switches itself off | Put a blanket over the TV and watch cartoons
test: Gran: "In my day we didn't have all these PINs and profiles." Why do families use them now? | Screens can reach many more films, so they help filter | PINs make films look better and brighter on new televisions | The law now forbids any TV without a PIN | Profiles help families pay less for their films
test: A friend at a sleepover: "Don't ring your mum, it's embarrassing, just watch it." What is the right choice? | Ring home or leave the room anyway | Watch it so the friend isn't upset | Pretend to watch with eyes closed | Go home without telling anybody
exam: Identify the two steps which this lesson prescribes in the event of a child encountering adult material on a screen. | Cessation of viewing, and notification of a trusted adult | Continuation of viewing, and notification of a friend | Reduction of volume, and concealment of the incident | Change of channel, and immediate dismissal from mind
exam: Determine the function of a personal identification number within the parental controls of a television service. | To restrict the playing of films above a chosen age | To reduce the cost of renting films above a certain age | To record the films watched for the purposes of school | To permit the playing of every film without restriction
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the efficacy of safe search. | It filters out much adult material but not all of it | It eliminates all adult material without exception | It has no effect whatsoever upon search results | It operates solely upon the spelling of searches
exam: Whereas a child has experienced such an accident, ascertain the statement concerning that child which this lesson affirms. | The accident does not alter who the child is | The child has become an adult as a consequence | The child must disclose it to the entire school | The child is to be excluded from viewing films
